Speaker 0: And that's the other thing.

578
00:45:43,860 --> 00:45:50,820
Speaker 0: She does remember certain things like if you stab her, that's going to affect how she goes in the neck.

579
00:45:51,542 --> 00:45:53,999
Speaker 0: Like decisions within the play through affect it.

580
00:45:54,180 --> 00:45:55,100
Speaker 0: Like, do you have the knife?

581
00:45:55,240 --> 00:45:56,060
Speaker 0: Do you not have the knife?

582
00:45:56,541 --> 00:46:01,180
Speaker 0: But specifically, the one that makes a big difference is, do you kill her?

583
00:46:01,300 --> 00:46:02,271
Speaker 0: You know, do you?

584
00:46:03,700 --> 00:46:08,660
Speaker 0: And often it surprises you with how that goes down.

585
00:46:08,720 --> 00:46:27,420
Speaker 1: What I appreciated too was when you start to when you figure out and learn in the game that you're in a construct and then the narrator is gone and everything, you realize that the long quiet and the background that appears every time you're by yourself or just with the narrator, it's very clearly your wings furled around yourself.

586
00:46:27,943 --> 00:46:33,380
Speaker 1: Because when the game ends, if you leave, then it describes like pressing like feathers.

587
00:46:33,960 --> 00:46:37,759
Speaker 1: You fan your wings out and you feel a barrier and you break through it like you're breaking out of an egg.

588
00:46:38,560 --> 00:46:43,280
Speaker 1: And then you get this vague image of like a giant winged god out in the cosmos.

589
00:46:44,020 --> 00:46:46,240
Speaker 0: What is that quote from?

590
00:46:46,940 --> 00:46:49,277
Speaker 0: I forget who it's from, but it's in Utena.

591
00:46:49,840 --> 00:46:52,360
Speaker 0: Well, all the student council says it as they go up in the elevator.

592
00:46:52,640 --> 00:46:56,400
Speaker 1: Oh, that's from Herman S. Demian.

593
00:46:56,930 --> 00:46:57,440
Speaker 0: Yeah, Demian.

594
00:46:57,920 --> 00:47:03,140
Speaker 0: And it's basically like the chick must smash itself to be born.

595
00:47:04,320 --> 00:47:11,180
Speaker 1: From the story of a boyhood about a bird wrestling its way out of its egg.

596
00:47:11,360 --> 00:47:13,639
Speaker 1: And really it's about boys coming of age.

597
00:47:15,103 --> 00:47:16,760
Speaker 0: So I want to say we have that book.

598
00:47:16,882 --> 00:47:16,969
Speaker 0: Yeah.

599
00:47:17,321 --> 00:47:22,200
Speaker 1: I read it a long time ago when we were doing that show doing lit crit on Utena.

600
00:47:22,820 --> 00:47:22,913
Speaker 0: Yeah.

601
00:47:23,321 --> 00:47:32,280
Speaker 0: Anyway, there's another thing where it's like characters questioning their own reality and becoming more because they're kind of trapped within a cycle.

602
00:47:32,700 --> 00:47:48,039
Speaker 1: But ultimately in the end, I like that we both, when you're confronted with the full reality of the constructs and you have to decide what to do, we both basically decided to break out into absolute reality with the princess knowing everything she could be.

603
00:47:48,942 --> 00:47:52,660
Speaker 1: I think I'd rather... Go out alone.

604
00:47:52,800 --> 00:47:54,280
Speaker 1: And I wasn't going to stay in my egg forever.

605
00:47:55,266 --> 00:47:56,440
Speaker 0: But that's the question.

606
00:47:56,660 --> 00:47:58,430
Speaker 0: You know, it's like, would you rather live?

607
00:48:01,144 --> 00:48:02,600
Speaker 0: Would you rather live, period?

608
00:48:03,200 --> 00:48:07,679
Speaker 0: Like living is a certain amount of suffering, a certain amount of imperfection.

609
00:48:08,141 --> 00:48:15,240
Speaker 0: Would you rather like static, unchanging, angelic, like nothingness?

610
00:48:15,762 --> 00:48:18,580
Speaker 0: Or would you rather the messy chaos of existence?

611
00:48:18,721 --> 00:48:26,780
Speaker 1: Well, it goes further, too, because that is the same dichotomy that Neon Genesis Evangelion delves in in the original show.

612
00:48:27,461 --> 00:48:29,894
Speaker 1: And in the remake, it triples down on that.

613
00:48:29,954 --> 00:48:33,280
Speaker 1: when you get to see minor spoilers for Evangelion.

614
00:48:33,781 --> 00:48:39,480
Speaker 1: You basically see the universe as Gendo wants it and what it would be if Gendo achieved everything he wanted.

615
00:48:39,941 --> 00:48:44,600
Speaker 1: And it's just him alone in a giant, cool ship being a sad sack for all of eternity.

616
00:48:45,034 --> 00:48:45,240
Speaker 0: Yeah.

617
00:48:45,540 --> 00:48:53,620
Speaker 0: So it's basically the whole point of Neon Genesis Evangelion was basically, you know, the hedgehog's dilemma.

618
00:48:54,180 --> 00:48:58,100
Speaker 0: Vulnerability and letting people in often hurts you.

619
00:48:58,302 --> 00:48:59,480
Speaker 0: You know, it's difficult.

620
00:48:59,560 --> 00:49:02,960
Speaker 0: You can never truly connect because we're all individuals.

621
00:49:03,582 --> 00:49:07,360
Speaker 0: But you have to try to let people in.

622
00:49:07,440 --> 00:49:14,760
Speaker 0: You have to try to connect and be a part of the world instead of walling yourself off in your own little cell.

623
00:49:15,000 --> 00:49:37,759
Speaker 1: But the other thing it evokes very strongly is the the singularity versus duality concept that appears in a lot of philosophy and a lot of literature in the sense that some of what the construct says, depending on what you ask, basically implies that there was one god and the person who made the construct basically clove that god in two and wanted to kill half of it.

624
00:49:38,281 --> 00:49:40,449
Speaker 1: But that implies, too, that your choice is.

625
00:49:40,509 --> 00:49:50,519
Speaker 1: that is is also is there just one thing or are there two things that might not agree or possibly more things, which is very close to, again, what Evangelion was all out about?

626
00:49:51,380 --> 00:49:52,398
Speaker 0: Wouldn't you say that?

627
00:49:52,980 --> 00:50:00,340
Speaker 0: So if you consider that the player character is a god, what do you think they are god of?

628
00:50:00,942 --> 00:50:02,440
Speaker 1: That is complicated.

629
00:50:02,942 --> 00:50:12,600
Speaker 0: Yeah, like you you talked a lot about the mound, about being a singularity, about being natural selection, although what do you think you the player represents?

630
00:50:12,700 --> 00:50:15,760
Speaker 1: I mean, the game seems to imply that you are effectively death.

631
00:50:16,420 --> 00:50:17,949
Speaker 1: You are described as the long quiet.

632
00:50:21,280 --> 00:50:25,300
Speaker 0: Oh, you know, in some ways that really makes sense, because if the princess is...

633
00:50:25,360 --> 00:50:27,860
Speaker 1: Between every act, you go back to the long quiet.

634
00:50:27,980 --> 00:50:30,740
Speaker 1: At the end, the narrator calls you the long quiet.

635
00:50:30,841 --> 00:50:32,239
Speaker 1: It basically says that's your name.

636
00:50:32,880 --> 00:50:39,580
Speaker 0: In some ways, you could kind of think about it like, if there's no life, there's also no death.

637
00:50:39,902 --> 00:50:41,472
Speaker 0: You know, how do we end death?

638
00:50:41,653 --> 00:50:42,600
Speaker 0: by ending life?

639
00:50:43,240 --> 00:50:44,676
Speaker 1: So you can be immortal and unchanging.

640
00:50:45,880 --> 00:50:51,820
Speaker 0: Yeah, by getting rid of change, by getting rid of the living things, then there is no more death.

641
00:50:51,840 --> 00:50:54,660
Speaker 1: But also the mound can't evolve without death.

642
00:50:54,700 --> 00:51:02,479
Speaker 1: And that's why I come back to that natural selection idea, because it reminded me of that story that had a recent fairly well animated YouTube video, The Goddess of Everything Else.

643
00:51:03,180 --> 00:51:16,160
Speaker 1: And the idea that if the construct only works because you don't understand what's happening, once you understand it and you emerge into the world as a god, it's you and the mound are the two things in the universe effectively.

644
00:51:16,620 --> 00:51:21,119
Speaker 1: And at that point, you can interact with each other, but the mound is as immortal as you are.

645
00:51:22,060 --> 00:51:27,120
Speaker 1: So there can still be change without necessarily death, but that's a different mode of being.

646
00:51:27,221 --> 00:51:29,820
Speaker 1: It's almost like transcending the previous order.

647
00:51:30,880 --> 00:51:32,940
Speaker 0: Well, death changes life.

648
00:51:33,320 --> 00:51:39,400
Speaker 1: I mean, imagine a distant future where conscious sentience evolves without ever having to die, but changes radically.

649
00:51:39,961 --> 00:51:49,840
Speaker 1: And, you know, the classic story of the thousand year old elf, who is a fundamentally different person now, but is one contiguous chain of experience and memories, but it still changes.

650
00:51:50,240 --> 00:51:53,760
Speaker 1: But it's a different kind of death from, well, you're just dead.

651
00:51:54,520 --> 00:51:55,187
Speaker 0: Gone forever.

652
00:51:55,207 --> 00:51:56,520
Speaker 0: Think no thoughts anymore.

653
00:51:56,600 --> 00:51:58,271
Speaker 1: What child that you had.

654
00:51:58,412 --> 00:52:03,658
Speaker 1: or your memes, maybe infect other people down the road, but you don't get to experience any of that.

655
00:52:04,602 --> 00:52:05,539
Speaker 0: Interesting stuff.

656
00:52:06,549 --> 00:52:15,740
Speaker 1: And on that note, sketchily drawn visual novel, body horror, eldritch story game.

657
00:52:16,601 --> 00:52:18,898
Speaker 0: I think it was well worth under 20 bucks.

658
00:52:19,640 --> 00:52:24,500
Speaker 0: You know, I would definitely recommend this game if any of this stuff sounded interesting.

659
